    "reason": "The recursion was implemented exactly as SPEC/paper (theta_k = Pi(-u_{k-1}), u_k = u_{k-1}+eta_k grad, eta_k = eta0 k^-0.501, averaged output). The same recursion in deterministic full-batch form converges to theta*, and the warm-started stochastic average stays at theta* to ~1e-3 (see warm_start_converged). What makes the prescribed schedule slow is the geometry of this DGP+constraint: z is almost a function of y (corr 0.81), the DI constraint direction xt~ is within ~2-4 deg of the label direction, so the constrained optimum lies in a very shallow (tangent curvature ~0.058) low-signal region. The averaged-iterate transient decays like exp(-h eta0 k^(1-alpha)/(1-alpha)); with eta0=0.01 the transient factor is ~0.5 at T=1e5 and ~0.15 at T=5e5, hence the large distances; T~1.5e9 would be needed for 1e-3. So it is NOT a formula typo: it is a step-size / problem-conditioning mismatch. Note the paper's reported synthetic-DI mean DI ~0.025 is much smaller than |DI(theta*)|~0.05-0.11, consistent with the paper having used finite-T averages that are still far from theta* (the finite-T averages in the table also show small DI)."
  }
